---
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: ical-proxy
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: ical-proxy
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# Renders allow-all when the flag is false, so `netpol_baseline_enforced` is a real rollback
   lever. `| bool` for the string trap. Both explained in full in
   networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    # ADDED 2026-08-24 with the ical-proxy route narrowing. This app serves unauthenticated
    # private calendar feeds and has no path that is not sensitive content — /calendar{1,2,4}.ics
    # is all of it, and there is no health endpoint. Its isolation used to be the Docker
    # `homepage_private` network; the cluster equivalent was the route's ClientIP guard, which
    # was then widened to the whole pod CIDR so the Kuma prober could reach it. That dissolved
    # the isolation: any pod could read the calendars, demonstrated live with a 200 and 847,922
    # bytes.
    #
    # So the two real callers moved off the edge onto this ClusterIP path, where a podSelector
    # can actually tell them apart — which the route could not, since everything arriving through
    # Traefik carries Traefik's identity. baseline-ingress admits ONLY traefik, prometheus and
    # the node CIDRs, so without this policy that move would deny both callers outright.
    #
    # NetworkPolicies are additive, so this unions with baseline-ingress rather than replacing
    # it: Traefik keeps its access and the daniel-server-guarded route still works.
    - from:
        # Homepage's three calendar widgets.
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: homepage
        # The "iCal Proxy k8s" monitor, which probes /calendar1.ics.
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: uptime-kuma
      ports:
        - port: {{ ical_proxy_k8s_port | default(5000) }}
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
